  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several common ways to study retinal structure and function in mice, combining physiological, behavioral, and imaging approaches. Overall, it shows how researchers move from isolated retinal tissue to whole-animal measurements and microscopic analysis.\n\nSome methods directly record electrical activity. Patch clamp is illustrated as taking retinal cells from the eye, placing them in a dish, and measuring membrane voltage from individual neurons. A multi-electrode array instead records signals from many retinal cells at once on a patterned electrode chip. Visually evoked potentials measure responses in an anesthetized mouse while visual stimuli are presented on a screen, capturing activity along the visual pathway rather than from single cells.\n\nOther panels show in vivo functional tests. The pupillary reflex panel depicts light-driven constriction of the pupil, while the light–dark box represents a behavioral assay of light sensitivity or visual preference. Two-photon microscopy and optical coherence tomography provide imaging of the living eye or retina, with OCT appearing to give a cross-sectional retinal view.\n\nFinally, immunohistochemistry with confocal imaging shows postmortem labeling and high-resolution visualization of retinal cell morphology, revealing branching neuronal structures in detail.",
